earch1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Lower bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number ... DOI 10.1007/S00373-018-1991-8 MaRDI QID Q1733882 zbMATH Open OpenAlex Wikidata FDO\n# Lower bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1007/S00373-018-1991-8MaRDI QIDQ1733882zbMATH OpenOpenAlexWikidataFDO\n\nAuthors Maximilian Fürst, Dieter Rautenbach\n\nPublication date 21 March 2019\n\nPublished in Graphs and Combinatorics (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.11032\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nmatchingacyclic matchinguniquely restricted matching\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nEdge subsets with special properties (factorization, matching, partitioning, covering and packing, etc.) (05C70)\n\nAbstract: A matching in a graph is uniquely restricted if no other matching covers exactly the same set of vertices. We establish tight lower bounds on the maximum size of a uniquely restricted matching in terms of order, size, and maximum degree.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHigh-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/338806/high-girth-approximation-conjecture-for-uniquely-restricted)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; High-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number\n\nHigh-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number\n\nAbout 8 years old · traced to 2018\n\nFor a graph $G$G, let $\\nu(G)$ν(G) denote its matching number and $\\nu_{ur}(G)$ν u r​(G) its uniquely restricted matching number. For every positive real number $\\epsilon$ϵ and every positive integer $\\Delta$Δ, consider graphs of maximum degree at most $\\Delta$Δ and girth at least $g$g. High-girth approximation conjecture. There is some positive integer $g=g(\\epsilon,\\Delta)$g=g(ϵ,Δ) such that\n\n$$\\nu_{ur}(G)\\geq (1-\\epsilon)\\nu(G)$$ν u r​(G)≥(1−ϵ)ν(G)\n\nfor every graph $G$G of maximum degree at most $\\Delta$Δ and girth at least $g$g. The conjecture asserts that, for fixed maximum degree, sufficiently large girth makes the uniquely restricted matching number asymptotically as close as desired to the ordinary matching number; the source gives no resolution, so it remains open.\n\n## Primary source\n\nM. Fürst and D. Rautenbach, “Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number”, arXiv:1803.11032 (2018).\n\ngraph-theoryhigh-girth-graphsmatchings\n\n0 0\n\n3 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCycles as edge intersection hypergraphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/330844999_Cycles_as_edge_intersection_hypergraphs)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs ... Picking up a problem from arXiv:1901.06292, for $n \\ge 24$ we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if n is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph ${\\cal H}=(V,{\\cal E})$ with $\\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\rceil$ hyperedges and $EI({\\cal H}) = C_n$. ... Further, it is shown that there is a polynomial f(k) of degree less than or equal to 3 with the property that given any integer k(⩾ 2) there exists a finite family F(k) of graphs such that any graph G with minimum edge-degree at least f(k) is the intersection graph of a k-uniform linear hypergraph if and only if G has no induced subgraph isomorphic to a member of F(k). ... It is shown that the strong perfect graph conjecture holds for EPT graphs.\n\n# Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs\n\n  * February 2019\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1902.00396\n\nAuthors:\n\nMartin Sonntag\n\nMartin Sonntag\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nHanns-Martin Teichert\n\nHanns-Martin Teichert\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIf ${\\cal H}=(V,{\\cal E})$ is a hypergraph, its edge intersection hypergraph $EI({\\cal H})=(V,{\\cal E}^{EI})$ has the edge set ${\\cal E}^{EI}=\\{e_1 \\cap e_2 \\ |\\ e_1, e_2 \\in {\\cal E} \\ \\wedge \\ e_1 \\neq e_2 \\ \\wedge \\ |e_1 \\cap e_2 |\\geq2\\}$. Picking up a problem from arXiv:1901.06292, for $n \\ge 24$ we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if n is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph ${\\cal H}=(V,{\\cal E})$ with $\\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\rceil$ hyperedges and $EI({\\cal H}) = C_n$.\n\n\n## References (6)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nOn edge-intersection graphs of k-bend paths in grids\n\nThe class of edge intersection graphs of a collection of paths in a tree (EPT graphs) is investigated, where two paths edge intersect if they share an edge. The cliques of an EPT graph are characterized and shown to have strong Helly number 4. From this it is demonstrated that the problem of finding a maximum clique of an EPT graph can be solved in polynomial time. It is shown that the strong perfect graph conjecture holds for EPT graphs. Further complexity results follow from the observation that every line graph is an EPT graph. The class of EPT graphs is equivalent to the class of fundamental cycle graphs.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nEdge intersection graphs of linear 3-uniform hypergraphs.\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2005\n  * Electron Notes Discrete Math\n\n  * Pavel Skums\n  * S.V. Suzdal\n  * Regina Tyshkevich\n\nView\n\n  * M Sonntag\n  * H.-M Teichert\n\nM. Sonntag, H.-M. Teichert, Edge intersection hypergraphs -a new hypergraph concept, arXiv:1901.06292[math.CO] (2019) 1-15. (submitted for publication)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscussiones Mathematicae (https://bibliotekanauki.pl/articles/59882286.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; edge intersection hypergraphs of 3-uniform hypergraphs, e.g. as wheels and com- ... (see the proof of Theorem 16 where we dealt with the clique fusion in the form ... We conjecture that the solution of Problem 18 becomes diﬃcult for r > 3. ... is a 3-regular (and, if n is even, 6-uniform) hypergraph H = (V, E) with ⌈n\nEdge Intersection Hypergraphs\n125\nBesides cycles, trees and cacti, also other classes of graphs are known to be\nedge intersection hypergraphs of 3-uniform hypergraphs, e.g. as wheels and com-\nplete graphs with at least 5 and 4 vertices, respectively. Having this in mind, the\nclique-fusion (k-fusion, for k ≥1) may be an appropriate tool to construct other\nclasses of graphs being edge intersection hypergraphs of 3-uniform hypergraphs.\nRemember that in Section 5 we restricted ourselves on the iterated clique-\nfusion in the sense, that in each step we applied the 1-fusion only to two graphs\n(see the proof of Theorem 16 where we dealt with the clique fusion in the form\n(· · · ((G1 ⊕G2) ⊕G3) · · · ⊕Gp) ⊕Gp+1). The usage of the k-fusion in a more\ncommon sense, namely as G1 ⊕· · · ⊕Gr, where r > 2 holds (see the original\ndeﬁnition at the beginning of Section 4) could be interesting for further inves-\ntigations. We conjecture that — using the clique-fusion in this common sense\n— the construction of corresponding classes of edge intersection hypergraphs of\n3-uniform hypergraphs may be much more complicated.\nAn interesting question is to have a look at the number of hyperedges being\nnecessary to generate certain graphs as edge intersection hypergraphs.\nProblem 18. Let G be a class of graphs, r ≥3, n0 ∈IN+, n ≥n0 and Gn ∈G a\ngraph with n vertices. What is the minimum cardinality |E| of the edge set of an\nr-uniform hypergraph Hn = (V, E) with EI(Hn) = Gn?\nWe conjecture that the solution of Problem 18 becomes diﬃcult for r > 3. A\nbreadcrumb for this can be found in [9], where for n ≥24 we prove that there\nis a 3-regular (and, if n is even, 6-uniform) hypergraph H = (V, E) with ⌈n\n2 ⌉\nhyperedges and EI(H) = Cn.\nAnother direction for further investigations may be to drop the restriction\nonto the class of graphs and search for hypergraphs which are edge intersection\nhypergraphs.\nAcknowledgement\nThe authors would like to thank one the referees for his valuable suggestions and\nremarks.\nReferences\n[1] C. Berge, Graphs and Hypergraphs (North Holland, Amsterdam, 1973).\n[2] T. Biedl and M. Stern, On edge-intersection graphs of k-bend paths in grids, Discrete\nMath. Theor. Comput. Sci. 12 (2010) 1–12.\nhttps://doi.org/10.46298/dmtcs.482\n[3] K. Cameron, S. Chaplick and C.T. Hoang, Edge intersection graphs of L-shaped\npaths in grids, Discrete Appl. Math. 210 (2016) 185–194.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2015.01.039--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the hardness of deciding the equality of the induced and the uniquely restricted matching number (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/332004937_On_the_hardness_of_deciding_the_equality_of_the_induced_and_the_uniquely_restricted_matching_number)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; In this paper, we study the relationship between these two parameters and the matching number. ... In this paper we introduce a new, specialized type of matching which we call uniquely restricted matchings, originally motivated by the problem of determining a lower bound on the rank of a matrix having a specified zero/ non-zero pattern.\nIn this paper we introduce a new, specialized type of matching which we call uniquely restricted matchings, originally motivated by the problem of determining a lower bound on the rank of a matrix having a specified zero/ non-zero pattern. A uniquely restricted matching is defined to be a matching M whose saturated vertices induce a subgraph which has only one perfect matching, namely M itself. We introduce the two problems of recognizing a uniquely restricted matching and of finding a maximum uniquely restricted matching in a given graph, and present algorithms and complexity results for certain special classes of graphs. We demonstrate that testing whether a given matching M is uniquely restricted can be done in O(|M||E|) time for an arbitrary graph G=(V,E) and in linear time for cacti, interval graphs, bipartite graphs, split graphs and threshold graphs. The maximum uniquely restricted matching problem is shown to be NP-complete for bipartite graphs, split graphs, and hence for chordal graphs and comparability graphs, but can be solved in linear time for threshold graphs, proper interval graphs, cacti and block graphs. Key words. Graph theory, Perfect graphs, Design and analysis of algorithms, Matchings, Uniquely restricted matchings.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquivalent Formulation of Thomassen's Conjecture Using Tutte Paths in Claw-Free Graphs (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/jgt.70113)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; We continue studying Thomassen's conjecture (every 4-connected line graph has a Hamilton cycle) in the direction of a recently shown equivalence with Jackson's conjecture (every 2-connected claw-free graph has a Tutte cycle), and we extend the equivalent formulation as follows: In every connected claw-free graph, any two vertices are connected by a maximal path which is a Tutte path. ... We study Tutte paths in claw-free graphs in the direction of recent results on Tutte cycles and paths by Čada et al. [6] and Li et al. [7], which investigate the following conjecture formulated by Thomassen [8] (note that, for better orientation, quoted known results and statements are labeled with letters). ... In relation to Conjectures C and 1, we note that there are infinitely many claw-free graphs which are not line graphs of hypergraphs of rank at most 3, and vice versa (this follows, e.g., from [17, Theorem 1], and the vice versa is trivial).\n\nAdam Kabela, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nAdam Kabela\n\n  * kabela@kma.zcu.cz\n\n  * orcid.org/0000-0002-0430-1797\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, European Centre of Excellence NTIS - New Technologies for the Information Society, University of West Bohemia, Pilsen, Czech Republic\n\nCorrespondence: Adam Kabela (kabela@kma.zcu.cz)\n\nZdeněk Ryjáček, \n\nZdeněk Ryjáček\n\n  * orcid.org/0000-0002-9877-0825\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, European Centre of Excellence NTIS - New Technologies for the Information Society, University of West Bohemia, Pilsen, Czech Republic\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nPetr Vrána, \n\nPetr Vrána\n\n  * orcid.org/0000-0001-9246-474X\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, European Centre of Excellence NTIS - New Technologies for the Information Society, University of West Bohemia, Pilsen, Czech Republic\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nFirst published: 28 August 2026\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.70113Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nWe study Tutte paths in claw-free graphs in the direction of recent results on Tutte cycles and paths by Čada et al. [6] and Li et al. [7], which investigate the following conjecture formulated by Thomassen [8] (note that, for better orientation, quoted known results and statements are labeled with letters).\n\nConjecture A. ((Thomassen [[8]]))Every 4-connected line graph has a Hamilton cycle.\n\nConjecture A remains open, even though partial results and numerous equivalent formulations of the problem are known (see, e.g., the survey [9]). In the positive direction, Zhan [10] and independently Jackson [11] showed that every 7-connected line graph of a multigraph has a Hamilton cycle, and Kaiser and Vrána [12] showed that every 5-connected line graph of minimum degree at least 6 is Hamilton-connected.\n\nIn [13], Ryjáček introduced a closure operation on claw-free graphs (a recursive application of the local completion operation on particular vertices that turns a claw-free graph into a line graph while preserving Hamiltonicity), and showed that Conjecture A is equivalent to a seemingly stronger conjecture by Matthews and Sumner [14] saying that every 4-connected claw-free graph has a Hamilton cycle.\n\nTwo recent equivalent formulations of Conjecture A restate the problem in terms of Tutte cycles and Tutte paths. Conjecture B was stated by Jackson [15] and the equivalence was shown by Čada et al. [6] (using the closure technique of [13]). Recently, Li et al. [7] showed that the problem can be equivalently formulated as Conjecture C.\n\nConjecture B. ((Jackson [[15]]))Every 2-connected claw-free graph has a Tutte cycle.\n\nConjecture C. ((Li et al. [[7]]))For every pair of vertices $ $a,b$$ of a connected line graph of a hypergraph of rank at most 3, there is a maximal $ $(a,b)$$-path which is a Tutte path.\n\nAs the main result, we present the following reformulation of the problem (seemingly stronger than Conjecture B), and we show that they are, in fact, equivalent.\n\nConjecture 1.For every pair of vertices $ $a,b$$ of a connected claw-free graph, there is a maximal $ $(a,b)$$-path which is a Tutte path.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-09-16 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-09-16)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; We resolve four of their conjectures completely, in each case extending partial results already in the literature: those for the Goellnitz-Gordon product Q_8 = (q,q^7;q^8)_inf/(q^3,q^5;q^8)_inf (Conjecture 21), for Q_12 = (q,q^11;q^12)_inf/(q^5,q^7;q^12)_inf (Conjecture 24), and for the Borwein products G_7 and G_11 (Conjectures 20 and 23).\nConjecture 21 fails exactly on [beta, 8/3), where beta is approximately 2.66448; the threshold 8/3 is sharp, and the first counterexamples occur near n = 7.6 x 10^5. Conjecture 24 fails exactly on (delta_1, 0), where delta_1 = -0.64411... is a root of the 22nd coefficient polynomial. Conjecture 20 fails exactly on (delta_c, 5), where delta_c = 4.8735075853867342634... is a root of the 897th coefficient polynomial. All other stated ranges are proved. Parts of Conjecture 21 were settled independently by He and Li; what is new here is the range near delta = -1, where their threshold diverges. Each failure has the same mechanism: a leading circle-method amplitude vanishes, and a secondary term with the wrong sign overtakes it. The proofs are self-contained apart from classical facts. The cusp analysis is exact, via finite orbits of Weil representations; the Hardy-Ramanujan-Rademacher expansion is made fully explicit; and certified computations in exact and ball arithmetic handle the finite ranges and the degenerate regimes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpen Math Problems Claimed to Be Solved with AI (https://aimath.robertj1.com/)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; ### Bondy’s minimum-degree longest-cycle conjecture ... Bondy posed the conjecture in 1980. ... Official problem record ↗Discussion and formalization notes ↗ ... Research paper ↗\n### Bondy’s minimum-degree longest-cycle conjecture\n\nProblem statement\n\nIf a $k$k-connected $n$n-vertex graph satisfies $\\delta(G)\\geq[n+k(k-1)]/(k+1)$δ(G)≥[n+k(k−1)]/(k+1), must every path in $G-V(C)$G−V(C) have at most $k-1$k−1 vertices for every longest cycle $C$C?\n\nMazur’s manuscript claims a proof for every connectivity $k$k, adapting the Ma–Ning–Zhao framework and importing a residual-cycle theorem of Nikoghosyan. A separate adversarial audit reported no mathematical error, but the claim is not yet an accepted resolution.\n\nDetails and sources\n\nAI contribution\n\nThe author credits GPT-5.6 Pro with assistance in developing the candidate argument. The public page separates the manuscript, its proof architecture, and a later adversarial audit.\n\nProblem origin\n\nThe indexed source traces the problem to human-authored literature or a pre-existing problem registry.\n\nVerification\n\nAdversarial audit; no formal or specialist review\n\nCandidate proof page and audit boundary ↗\n\nClaim audit\n\nNot formally verified or peer reviewed. The candidate proof depends on external theorems whose exact applicability remains a critical specialist-review question.\n\nPublication\n\nPublic candidate manuscript and LaTeX source; no peer-reviewed publication or proof-assistant formalization\n\nPreprint / manuscript\n\nCandidate proof manuscript ↗June 2026 sufficiently-large-graphs preprint ↗\n\nActivity evidence\n\nBondy posed the conjecture in 1980. It is known for $k\\leq3$k≤3, and a June 2026 paper proved it for all sufficiently large graphs, showing active expert progress immediately before this candidate appeared.\n\nA June 2026 human preprint proves Bondy’s conjecture for all sufficiently large graphs but explicitly leaves the general $k\\geq4$k≥4 case open. ProofAtlas continues to label this later manuscript a candidate and says its use of the Ma–Ning–Zhao and Nikoghosyan inputs, novelty, and historical relationship require specialist checking.\n\nVerification\n\nAuthor-checked paper; official record updated\n\nPublication\n\nPublic proof manuscript and official problem record\n\nPreprint / manuscript\n\nProof manuscript ↗\n\nActivity evidence\n\nOne original source and a small public discussion were located; the score is a conservative editorial estimate.\n\nA forum Lean experiment formalizes a conditional route using an Erdős–Rado-style input; it is not an unconditional end-to-end formal proof and is therefore not counted as such.\n\nOfficial problem record ↗Discussion and formalization notes ↗\n\nNo finite uniform bound\n\nClaimed outcome\n    Disproved\n\nProblem origin\n    Human-source problem\n\nSystem\n    GPT-5.4 Pro + Przemek Chojecki\n\nVerification\n    Author-checked paper; official record updated\n\nOpen for\n    39 years\n\nResearch activity*\n    1/5\n\n### Rota’s matroid-flat unimodality conjecture\n\nProblem statement\n\nFor every matroid, is the sequence counting flats of each rank unimodal?\n\nThe paper constructs local valleys in the number of flats by rank using a $q$q-lift mechanism, producing explicit counterexamples to the conjectured unimodality.\n\nDetails and sources\n\nAI contribution\n\nAt the authors’ suggestion the model found a seed example for a weaker reciprocal-convexity question. The human authors generalized the mechanism and wrote the mathematical paper.\n\nProblem origin\n\nThe indexed source traces the problem to human-authored literature or a pre-existing problem registry.\n\nVerification\n\nAuthor-checked explicit construction\n\nPublication\n\nPublic arXiv manuscript; peer review pending\n\nPreprint / manuscript\n\nResearch paper ↗\n\nActivity evidence\n\nThe conjecture is attributed to Rota and sits inside a central, highly active program on log-concavity and matroid invariants.\n\nThe explicit construction disproves the universal unimodality claim. Minimal counterexample questions and broader structural classifications remain open.\n\nConjecture disproved\n\nClaimed outcome\n    Disproved\n\nProblem origin\n    Human-source problem\n\nSystem\n    ChatGPT-5.6 Pro\n\nVerification\n    Author-checked explicit construction\n\nOpen for\n    56 years\n\nResearch activity*\n    5/5\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nÉvénements | Institut des sciences mathématiques (https://www.ism.uqam.ca/accueil/evenements/)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Based on computational evidence, we formulate a new conjecture. ... Non-reversible parallel tempering (NRPT) is an effective algorithm for sampling from target distributions with complex geometry, such as those arising from posterior distributions of weakly identifiable and high-dimensional Bayesian models.\n\n## Colloque des sciences mathématiques du Québec - Katherine Stange, University of Colorado, Boulder\n\nvendredi 2 février 2024\n\nThe local-global conjecture for Apollonian circle packings is false\n\nPrimitive integral Apollonian circle packings are fractal arrangements of tangent circles with integer curvatures.  The curvatures form an orbit of a 'thin group,' a subgroup of an algebraic group having infinite index in its Zariski closure.  The curvatures that appear must fall into one of six or eight residue classes modulo 24. The twenty-year old local-global conjecture states that every sufficiently large integer in one of these residue classes will appear as a curvature in the packing. We prove that this conjecture is false for many packings, by proving that certain quadratic and quartic families are missed. The new obstructions are a property of the thin Apollonian group (and not its Zariski closure), and are a result of quadratic and quartic reciprocity, reminiscent of a Brauer-Manin obstruction. Based on computational evidence, we formulate a new conjecture.  This is joint work with Summer Haag, Clyde Kertzer, and James Rickards.  Time permitting, I will discuss some new results, joint with Rickards, that extend these phenomena to certain settings in the study of continued fractions.\n\nDate: 2 février 2024, 15h30\n\nLieu: Centre de recherches mathématiques, Pavillon André-Aisenstadt, Université de Montréal, Salle 5340\n\n## Colloque des sciences mathématiques du Québec - Trevor Campbell, UBC\n\nvendredi 26 janvier 2024\n\nAn exploration-agnostic characterization of the ergodicity of parallel tempering\n\nNon-reversible parallel tempering (NRPT) is an effective algorithm for sampling from target distributions with complex geometry, such as those arising from posterior distributions of weakly identifiable and high-dimensional Bayesian models. In this talk I will establish the uniform geometric ergodicity of NRPT under an efficient local exploration hypothesis, which avoids the intricacies of dealing with kernel-specific properties. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Furst_M in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AFurst_M+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In the present paper, we provide best possible upper bounds on $\\gamma_e(G)$ for regular and non-regular graphs $G$ in terms of their order and maximum degree. ...   * On the hardness of deciding the equality of the induced and the uniquely restricted matching number\nFurthermore, we discuss algorithmic consequences of our results and their constructive proofs.\n\n  * On the hardness of deciding the equality of the induced and the uniquely restricted matching number\n\nMaximilian Fürst\n\nDec 24 2018 math.CO arXiv:1812.09038v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1812.09038, author = {Maximilian Fürst}, title = {{O}n the hardness of deciding the equality of the induced and the uniquely restricted matching number}, year = {2018}, eprint = {1812.09038}, note = {arXiv:1812.09038v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIf $G(M)$ denotes the subgraph of a graph $G$ induced by the set of vertices that are covered by some matching $M$ in $G$, then $M$ is an induced or a uniquely restricted matching if $G(M)$ is $1$-regular or if $M$ is the unique perfect matching of $G(M)$, respectively. Let $\\nu_s(G)$ and $\\nu_{ur}(G)$ denote the maximum cardinality of an induced and a uniquely restricted matching in $G$. Golumbic, Hirst, and Lewenstein (Uniquely restricted matchings, Algorithmica 31 (2001) 139-154) posed the problem to characterize the graphs $G$ with $\\nu_{ur}(G) = \\nu_{s}(G)$. We prove that the corresponding decision problem is NP-hard, which suggests that a good characterization is unlikely to be possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Parameterized Perspective on Uniquely Restricted Matchings (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/394540000_A_Parameterized_Perspective_on_Uniquely_Restricted_Matchings)\nciteturn2search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; applications—not just in matrix theory [2], but also in modeling compatibility among a group of ... The uniquely restricted matching number of a graph G is the maximum cardinality of a uniquely ... tee parameters of the form UB−ℓ, where UB is an upper bound on the uniquely restricted matching\nAddition-\n\nally, an intriguing direction is to investigate the potential of the clique-width parameter, which has\n\nrecently been studied in the context of related problems like Induced Matching and Acyclic\n\nMatching [7]. It will be interesting to identify whether the same techniques can be extended to\n\nUniquely Restricted Matching. Another promising area of research involves studying guaran-\n\ntee parameters of the form UB−ℓ, where UB is an upper bound on the uniquely restricted matching\n\nnumber. This approach has been explored in the context of Induced Matching and Acyclic\n\nMatching [4, 12], and it appears particularly relevant given that the problem is W[1]-hard with\n\nrespect to the solution size ℓ.\n\n1 Two sets A and B are incomparable if neither A⊆B nor B⊆A.\n\n4\n\n1.4 Organization of the Paper\n\nIn Section 2, we present the deﬁnitions and concepts that will be used throughout the paper.\n\nSection 3 focuses on the Uniquely Restricted Matching problem on line graphs, with solution\n\nsize as the parameter. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachina Mathematica (https://lean.up.railway.app/)\nciteturn2search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; He conjectured that every odd nonsquare $n$ with $\\sigma(n)\\ge 2n$ is $\\{-1,1\\}$-perfect, with computational evidence to $10^{4}$; the conjecture was disproved by Campbell in July 2026, who produced counterexamples $ap$ with $a$ an odd square, $\\sigma(a)\\ge 2a$, and $p>\\sigma(a)$ prime. ... EGR7  | routine  | λ ≤ (k-1)² for girth 4, and the nonexistence statements it gives at every order simultaneously ... EGR12  | routine  | The literature lower bound for n(k,g,λ) at girth 3 and 4 — Drglin–Filipovski–Jajcay–Raiman's Theorem 2.3, the formula that generates that column of the published tables ... recip-06  | routine  | Myers-Parrish's f_2(5) = 39 refuted, with no search\n## Papers\n\npm1-perfect · math.NT · topic: Arithmetic functions & integer sequences · v1 · pdf\n\n$\\{-1,1\\}$-perfect numbers: a certified window to $10^{6}$, seven negative signs, and the least counterexample to Ross's Conjecture 17\n\nAbstract.Ross calls $n$ an $\\SSS$-perfect number of the first kind when the divisors of $n$ strictly between $1$ and $n$ can be given coefficients from $\\SSS$ so that $1+\\sum_{j}\\lambda_{j}d_{j}=n$; for $\\SSS=\\{-1,1\\}$ this is a signed-divisor, or subset-sum, condition. He conjectured that every odd nonsquare $n$ with $\\sigma(n)\\ge 2n$ is $\\{-1,1\\}$-perfect, with computational evidence to $10^{4}$; the conjecture was disproved by Campbell in July 2026, who produced counterexamples $ap$ with $a$ an odd square, $\\sigma(a)\\ge 2a$, and $p>\\sigma(a)$ prime. Neither paper bounds the least counterexample. We prove, by exhibiting and re-checking a signing certificate for each of the $1989$ admissible $n$, that every odd nonsquare $n\\le 10^{6}$ with $\\sigma(n)\\ge 2n$ is $\\{-1,1\\}$-perfect — a hundred times the published range — and, in the same sweep, that seven negative coefficients always suffice there, a refinement neither source considers and one that is not a theorem in waiting: the minimum number of negative coefficients needed is already $29$ somewhere below $2.6\\cdot 10^{8}$. We give a self-contained modulus certificate refuting $n_{0}=253\\,277\\,325=3^{2}5^{2}7^{2}\\cdot 22973$, which is the least member of Campbell's construction and is about $6810$ times smaller than the instance he prints; and we record an exhaustive search, run twice in independent implementations and outside the formal development, which finds $n_{0}$ to be the least counterexample of any shape and finds exactly $61$ counterexamples below $2.6\\cdot 10^{8}$, all of the form $11025p$ with $p$ prime, $22973\\le p\\le 23581$. Ross's two printed lists are reproduced and completed over $n\\le 80$. All statements below except those explicitly labelled as computations outside the development are machine-checked in Lean 4; \\S\\ref{sec:verif} says exactly what the machine checks. We also record plainly that the arXiv posting arXiv:2512.04417 of Ross's paper is a republication of the 2024 journal article and that the two texts differ: the journal prints the verification range $10^{4}$, the posting prints no range at all.\n\n"